The SDEF component is a data definition language, roughly corresponding to
original CDC *.dtpinc files. SDEF is used only for data definitions (i.e. structs),
never for actual data.

SOBJs are used for data. The tree for an SOBJ closely follows its definition
(SDEF)'s tree. Here's how their CDC equivalents look like:

    [...]
    <Item Name="87869B946401183_cp10">
        <Item Name="Meta">
            <Item Name="Pos" Value="-115.957794,309.110321"/>
        </Item>
        <Item Name="ActiongraphNode">
            <Item Name="NodeType" Value="Misc_FrameTrigger"/>
            <Item Name="ActiongraphNodeProperties">
                <Item Name="m_enabled" Value="false"/>
            </Item>
        </Item>
    </Item>
    <Item Name="878617646401183_cp11">
        <Item Name="Meta">
            <Item Name="Pos" Value="-117.623779,279.358826"/>
        </Item>
        <Item Name="ActiongraphNode">
            <Item Name="ActiongraphNodeProperties">
                <Item Name="Desc" Value="Disabled:  for debugging"/>
            </Item>
        </Item>
    </Item>
    [...]
    (Source: excerpt from TRAS_Nov16_2 laracroft.drm)

The equivalent SDEF/dtpinc would look like:

    <Module Name="cdc.dtp.actiongraph.actiongraph">
        <Struct Name="ActionGraph">
            <Enum Name="NodeEnum" Underlying="int16">
                [...]
                <EnumValue Name="Misc_FrameTrigger" Value="309" />
                [...]
            </Enum>

            <!-- Like tagged union -->
            <Variant Name="NodeData" Nameless="True">
                [...]
                <!-- FrameTriggerNode is a struct -->
                <VariantType EnumValue="Misc_FrameTrigger" Value="FrameTriggerNode" />
                [...]
            </Variant>

            <Struct Name="ActiongraphNodeType">
                <Field Name="NodeType" Type="NodeEnum" />
                <Field Name="ActiongraphNodeProperties" Type="NodeData" VariantTypeField="NodeType" />
            </Struct>

            [...]

            <Struct Name="Node">
                <Field Name="ActiongraphNode" Type="ActiongraphNodeType" />
                <Field Name="DebugFlags" Type="GenGraphNodeDebugFlags" />
                <Field Name="m_debugName" Type="string" />
            </Struct>

            [...]

            <Field Name="NumNodes" Type="uint32" />
            <Field Name="NumEdges" Type="uint32" />
            <Field Name="NodeList" Type="Node*" />
            <Field Name="EdgeList" Type="Edge*" />
            <Field Name="NumInputs" Type="uint32" />
            <Field Name="NumOutputs" Type="uint32" />
            <Field Name="Inputs" Type="ScriptableNodeInputSocket*" />
            <Field Name="Outputs" Type="GraphSocket*" />
            <Field Name="IntroPanelInput" Type="GraphsInputsStruct" />
            <Field Name="IntroPanelOutput" Type="GraphsOutputsStruct" />
            <Bitfield>
                <Field Name="RTEFlag" Type="uint8" BitWidth="1" />
            </Bitfield>
        </Struct>
    </Module>

Some assumptions about how CDC does stuff:
 - The root "Item" contains the type (I think in CDC this would be called the "dtp")
   of the root subsection, like:
       <Item Name="ActionGraph" Type="ActionGraph">
   This would make sense, given that for DATPath in gamegui links we have:
       $<Link Caption=\"%s\" Category=\"dtp\" DATID=\"%s\" DATPath=\"ActionGraph.NodeList.%s\"/>$
   Starting with "ActionGraph".

